WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.510
My class built a sensor for

2
00:00:02.510 --> 00:00:03.190
the school garden.

3
00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:05.650
On my desk, it worked perfectly.

4
00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:07.930
Out in the garden: nothing.

5
00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:10.310
The problem wasn't the sensor.

6
00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:12.810
It was how it talked.

7
00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:16.120
Today: how connected things talk,

8
00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:18.060
and where your data goes.

9
00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:22.190
Connected things send what they sense by

10
00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:24.710
radio, and every radio makes a trade.

11
00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:26.500
How far does it reach?

12
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:28.760
How much battery does it use?

13
00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:31.120
How much data can it carry?

14
00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:34.790
Bluetooth reaches about ten to thirty

15
00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:37.870
metres, usually to a phone, and sips

16
00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:38.190
power.

17
00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:41.520
Wi-Fi reaches thirty to fifty metres

18
00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:44.700
indoors and carries lots of data, even

19
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:47.980
video, but needs a router and plenty of

20
00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:48.260
power.

21
00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:51.750
For long distances, a mobile network

22
00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:54.150
works anywhere with coverage, but

23
00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:55.930
needs a SIM and a data plan.

24
00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:59.420
LoRa reaches two to five kilometres in a

25
00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:02.060
town, and ten or more in open country.

26
00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:05.580
It sends a few dozen bytes at a time, on

27
00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.760
very little power, to a gateway.

28
00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:09.650
Quick check!

29
00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:14.580
A farm rain gauge sends one small reading

30
00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:17.820
an hour to an office three kilometres

31
00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:20.980
away, with no power in the field.

32
00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:22.520
Which radio?

33
00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:24.360
LoRa.

34
00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:27.150
Three kilometres is too far for

35
00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:29.250
Bluetooth or Wi-Fi, and a

36
00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:30.750
reading is only a few bytes.

37
00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:34.230
LoRa carries it that far on so little

38
00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:36.930
power that a battery lasts a year.

39
00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:39.710
Now follow one reading.

40
00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:43.110
The sensor sends it to a gateway, which

41
00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:45.190
passes radio messages to the internet.

42
00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:47.710
The internet carries it to a cloud

43
00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:50.230
server, a computer in a data centre.

44
00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:52.890
Then an app on your phone shows it.

45
00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:56.950
At every step, someone else handles your

46
00:01:56.950 --> 00:02:00.030
reading: the router's owner, the internet

47
00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:03.330
company, the cloud company, the app maker.

48
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.060
A garden's temperature is harmless.

49
00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:09.910
Your location or your home camera is not.

50
00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:13.310
So data should travel encrypted:

51
00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:16.830
scrambled, so only the two ends can read

52
00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:16.950
it.

53
00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:19.740
A website's padlock, https,

54
00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:22.160
does exactly that.

55
00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:25.030
But the app at the end still reads it

56
00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:27.430
all, so choose apps you trust.

57
00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:29.340
Your turn.

58
00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:32.460
Aisha's family camera sends video

59
00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.380
to an app with no encryption.

60
00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:36.700
Who could watch it on the way?

61
00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:39.940
Anyone on the way could: without

62
00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:42.220
encryption, everyone in the middle can see

63
00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:42.400
it.

64
00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:45.000
Remember: radios trade

65
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.360
range, power and data.

66
00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:49.930
Bluetooth and Wi-Fi are short;

67
00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:52.490
mobile and LoRa go far.

68
00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:54.870
And lock your data on the way.

69
00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:59.130
Try it tomorrow: a radio hunt at home.

70
00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:02.650
List five connected things, like

71
00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:05.930
a TV, a router or earbuds.

72
00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:08.470
Guess how each talks:

73
00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:10.870
Bluetooth, Wi-Fi or mobile.

74
00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:12.920
Then check its settings.

75
00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:15.580
Next time: mesh networks.
